introduction:
  bigQuestion: "How do we handle Data at Global Scale?"
  overview: "This unit prepares you for the world of <strong>Big Data</strong>. You will move far beyond simple tables to explore <strong>Distributed Systems</strong>, <strong>NoSQL</strong> architectures, and the complex trade-offs between <strong>Consistency</strong> (ACID) and <strong>Availability</strong> (CAP Theorem)."
  journey: "You'll start by mastering critical reliability with <strong>Transactions (ACID)</strong>. Then you'll break the single-server limit with <strong>Distributed Databases</strong>, before exploring modern alternatives like <strong>NoSQL</strong> and <strong>Data Mining</strong>."

concepts:
  - title: "Transactions (ACID)"
    icon: "fa-shield-halved"
    content: |
      <p>Banking relies on this. A group of operations (Transfer: -100 here, +100 there) must succeed or fail as a UNIT.</p>
      <ul>
          <li><strong>Atomicity:</strong> All or nothing.</li>
          <li><strong>Consistency:</strong> The DB remains valid.</li>
          <li><strong>Isolation:</strong> Transactions don't interfere.</li>
          <li><strong>Durability:</strong> Once saved, it's saved forever.</li>
      </ul>

  - title: "Distributed Databases"
    icon: "fa-network-wired"
    content: |
      <p>When one server isn't enough. We split the data across the world.</p>
      <ul>
          <li><strong>Replication:</strong> Copying data to multiple locations (Safety).</li>
          <li><strong>Fragmentation:</strong> Splitting a table into pieces (Speed).</li>
          <li><strong>Trade-off:</strong> Keeping copies in sync is HARD (CAP Theorem).</li>
      </ul>

  - title: "NoSQL & Modern Models"
    icon: "fa-file-code"
    content: |
      <p>SQL is rigid. Sometimes we need flexibility.</p>
      <ul>
          <li><strong>Document Stores (JSON):</strong> Flexible structure (e.g., MongoDB).</li>
          <li><strong>Key-Value:</strong> Ultra-fast lookups (e.g., Redis).</li>
          <li><strong>Graph:</strong> Storing relationships (e.g., Social Networks).</li>
      </ul>

  - title: "Data Intelligence (Mining)"
    icon: "fa-chart-pie"
    content: |
      <p>Turning raw data into knowledge.</p>
      <ul>
          <li><strong>Data Warehouse:</strong> A massive archive of historical data.</li>
          <li><strong>ETL:</strong> Extract, Transform, Load (Cleaning the data).</li>
          <li><strong>Data Mining:</strong> Using algorithms to find hidden patterns (e.g., "People who buy diapers also buy beer").</li>
      </ul>

terminology:
  - term: "Transaction"
    definition: "A sequence of database operations treated as a single logical unit."
  - term: "ACID"
    definition: "The set of properties (Atomicity, Consistency, Isolation, Durability) that guarantee database reliability."
  - term: "Data Warehouse"
    definition: "A large, centralized repository of data integrated from disparate sources, used for analysis."
  - term: "Data Mining"
    definition: "The process of discovering patterns, correlations, and anomalies within large datasets."
  - term: "Distributed Database"
    definition: "A database in which storage devices are not all attached to a common processor (geographic spread)."
  - term: "Replication"
    definition: "The process of sharing information to ensure consistency between redundant resources."
  - term: "NoSQL"
    definition: "Non-relational databases designed for unrelated, unstructured, or rapidly changing data."
  - term: "OLAP"
    definition: "Online Analytical Processing. Technology used to organize large business databases and support complex analysis."

misconceptions:
  - mistake: "NoSQL is always faster"
    correction: "It can be faster for simple reads/writes, but it often lacks the strict safety guarantees (ACID) of SQL."
  - mistake: "Data Mining = Searching"
    correction: "Searching finds a known record. Mining finds UNKNOWN patterns (e.g., predicting customer churn)."
  - mistake: "Distributed means Instant"
    correction: "Speed of light is real. Updating a copy in Tokyo takes time to reach New York (Eventual Consistency)."

